CORE REQUIREMENT / REFRAME (Peter 2026-08-09, said plainly): 'I don't want to jump, and I want a browser and terms.' Two durable requirements that keep resurfacing: (1) NO JUMP — movement between things must be continuous/spatial, never a disorienting whole-screen swap (same 'I get lost between pages' pain from session 3 + the pan-not-flip idea + edge-scroll idea — they're all THIS). (2) BROWSER + TERMS COEXIST — must see a web browser AND strand terms at once (QR-lookup was one instance; it's general). BOTH trace to one root: the FULLSCREEN-TMUX-DECK model owns the whole screen and can't share it with a browser, and tmux pages swap with a jump. This reframes the strand: the question isn't 'how to edge-scroll' — it's 'WHAT SURFACE holds both a browser and terms with no jumps?' Candidates (a design fork, don't rush): (A) non-fullscreen tiled deck + browser side by side (simplest, loses immersion, no jump because nothing swaps — just look L/R); (B) terms as REAL windows WM-tiled with the browser (kills tmux-pane-can't-leave, loses single-surface identity model); (C) a browser inside the deck (terminal browser / web view — keeps one surface but bad for viewing a QR picture). NOTE: neither tmux (no viewport) nor xfwm4 (no active-edge; wrap_workspaces is window-drag only) has native cursor-edge-scroll — it'd be a grubby xdotool getmouselocation polling daemon, which is a WORKAROUND for the wrong container, not the fix. Solve the container question first.
